INT. FAMILY HARDWARE STORE - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

A narrow hardware store, aisles crowded floor to ceiling with bins of nails, rope, washers, paint cans. A single bulb hangs over the register. A cardboard sign taped to the door reads: "CLOSED. FOREVER?"

ROSE LINDQVIST, sixties, the widow, sits on a stool at the register, a cigar box open in front of her. HENRY LINDQVIST, forties, her eldest, in a suit, paces the center aisle. MARGO LINDQVIST-BYRNE, thirties, her daughter, leans against the paint counter with her arms crossed. DANNY LINDQVIST, twenty-five, her youngest, sits on an upturned bucket by the door. UNCLE FRITZ, seventies, Rose's brother-in-law, polishes a hammer with a rag, saying nothing.

HENRY
Mom, the offer expires Friday.

ROSE
I know when Friday is.

MARGO
Henry, can you sit down?

HENRY
I'm trying to help.

MARGO
You're trying to pace.

HENRY
Danny, tell her.

DANNY
Tell her what?

HENRY
That fourteen hundred square feet next to a Whole Foods is worth three times what this place makes.

DANNY
I don't know, Henry.

HENRY
You don't know.

DANNY
It's Dad's store.

HENRY
Dad's dead, Danny.

Silence. Fritz stops polishing. Margo uncrosses her arms. Rose closes the cigar box with a small click.

ROSE
Say that again.

HENRY
Mom, I didn't mean...

ROSE
Say it again, Henry. To my face.

Henry stops in the middle of the aisle. He opens his mouth. He closes it.

MARGO
Mom.

ROSE
Quiet, Margo.

Danny stands. The bucket tips and clatters. Henry spins toward the sound, his sleeve catching a display of screwdrivers, and a hundred handles hit the floor, rolling.

HENRY
Great. Fantastic.

DANNY
I've got it.

HENRY
Leave it, Danny!

DANNY
I said I've got it!

He drops to his knees and gathers the screwdrivers. Margo kneels beside him. Henry stands with his hands open at his sides. Fritz sets down the hammer, picks up a broom, and begins to sweep, slow and steady.

ROSE
Your father kept the books in this box.

She lifts the lid. Inside are index cards, hundreds of them, in neat blue ink.

ROSE (CONT'D)
Every customer who ever couldn't pay. Every tab he let ride. Forty years.

MARGO
Mom, I never knew.

ROSE
Nobody did. That's the thing about your father. He never wanted to be thanked.

HENRY
(quiet)
How much is owed?

ROSE
I haven't counted. I think it's more than the Whole Foods offer.

Henry laughs once, a helpless sound. He sits down on the floor in the middle of the screwdrivers.

HENRY
Uncle Fritz, did you know?

FRITZ
I wrote half those cards.

DANNY
We're not selling.

HENRY
Danny.

DANNY
We're not selling it, Henry.

MARGO
No.

HENRY
(rising, pulling at his tie)
Then we run it. All four of us. Like he did.

ROSE
Five.

She nods at Fritz. He lifts the broom in salute. Henry crosses to the register and takes the cigar box in both hands, and Rose lets him.
